{
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Support baseline: the .NET SDK 11 or newer, and `net11.0` is the only
  // target framework the repository builds against.
  //
  // The package itself targets netstandard2.0, so the *compiled assembly* runs
  // on .NET Framework 4.7.2+, .NET Core 2.0+ and .NET 5+ - that is a runtime
  // statement. Building it needs SDK 11: the projects declare `net11.0` and
  // nothing else, so an SDK 10 cannot build them at all. There is no SDK 8 /
  // SDK 9 / SDK 10 promise any more, and no Roslyn 4.8 one either (see
  // docs/release-notes/batch4.md). The bundled source generator is still
  // *compiled* against the Roslyn 5.9 floor - the compiler SDK 10 shipped - and
  // a 5.9-built analyzer loads in every later compiler, SDK 11's included, so
  // the analyzer keeps reaching consumers on a wider compiler range than the
  // one this repository is built with.
  //
  // NO SDK is pinned here, deliberately. The floor is declared where it belongs
  // now: the target framework (`net11.0`) states which SDK must be able to
  // build the repository, so an SDK is never a number in this file.
  // Reproducibility is CI's job instead - every job sets up the 11.0.x leg
  // explicitly (DOTNET_VERSION in .github/workflows/ci.yml), so the pipeline
  // builds and tests on one known SDK whatever the runner image carries.
  //
  // History, for the reader who remembers the block that used to be here: it
  // declared `version: 10.0.100` with `rollForward: latestMajor` (plus
  // `allowPrerelease: false`), and latestMajor was the only thing that let a
  // pinned 10.0.100 accept SDK 11. With the pin gone that escape hatch has
  // nothing left to escape, so all three fields went with it.
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "test": {
    "runner": "Microsoft.Testing.Platform"
  }
}
